#
# Declarative competency questions for the slice-quality-rubric slice
# (crates/slicetest). The QC cells find classes of structural incompleteness in
# the resident rubric ABox; for those a non-empty result is a failure, so each
# pins the empty set. The last cell is the complement — a question whose answer
# is a term, pinned as a positive roster BY IRI. Every cell runs in the default
# asserted lane on purpose: reasoning must not be able to invent the missing
# binding/descriptor or the missing declaration, so the guard is meaningful only
# over what is actually authored. Each rationale states the ontological reason
# and names no test artifact.

@prefix gmeow: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/> .
@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/slice-quality-rubric/tests/> .

ex:cqAxesBindProducers a gmeow:CompetencyQuestion ;
    gmeow:cqQueryFile "slices/core/slice-quality-rubric/queries/qc/axes-without-producer.rq" ;
    gmeow:cqRationale "Every quality axis must bind a measurement primitive with gmeow:axisProducer, because an axis the kernel cannot resolve to a primitive is unscorable; the query that finds producerless axes must return the empty set." ;
    gmeow:cqResultShape ex:cqAxesBindProducersShape ;
    gmeow:cqExpectRowCount 0 .

ex:cqAxesBindProducersShape a logic:ResultShape ;
    logic:declaresColumn
        [ logic:columnVariable "axis" ; logic:columnTermKind logic:TermKindIri ; logic:columnBinding logic:BindingRequired ] ;
    logic:shapeCardinality logic:RowsCount ;
    logic:shapeRowCount 0 .

# The QC query (queries/qc/projection-ceilings.rq) finds any committed
# gmeow:ProjectionCeilingCommitment whose gmeow:ceilingVocabulary fails to
# declare a complete descriptor (gmeow:vocabularyDefaultCeiling,
# gmeow:vocabularySubsumedBy, gmeow:vocabularyPreservation); a non-empty result
# is a failure, so the cell pins the empty set. It runs in the default asserted
# lane on purpose: reasoning must not be able to invent a missing descriptor
# triple, so the completeness guard is meaningful only over what is actually
# authored.
ex:cqProjectionCeilingsHold a gmeow:CompetencyQuestion ;
    gmeow:cqQueryFile "slices/core/slice-quality-rubric/queries/qc/projection-ceilings.rq" ;
    gmeow:cqRationale "Every committed projection ceiling names a vocabulary, and Principle 17 requires that vocabulary to carry its own descriptor — a default ceiling, a logic: subsumption witness, and a preservation judgment — or the loss-ledger audit trail is broken for a vocabulary actually in use; the query that finds committed ceilings referencing an incompletely-described vocabulary must return the empty set." ;
    gmeow:cqResultShape ex:cqProjectionCeilingsHoldShape ;
    gmeow:cqExpectRowCount 0 .

ex:cqProjectionCeilingsHoldShape a logic:ResultShape ;
    logic:declaresColumn
        [ logic:columnVariable "ceiling" ; logic:columnTermKind logic:TermKindIri ; logic:columnBinding logic:BindingRequired ] ,
        [ logic:columnVariable "slice" ; logic:columnTermKind logic:TermKindIri ; logic:columnBinding logic:BindingRequired ] ,
        [ logic:columnVariable "vocab" ; logic:columnTermKind logic:TermKindIri ; logic:columnBinding logic:BindingRequired ] ;
    logic:shapeCardinality logic:RowsCount ;
    logic:shapeRowCount 0 .

# The one cell in this file that pins a POSITIVE roster rather than the empty set: the
# question asks which property a consumer joins a grade to its axis on, and the answer is
# a term, not an absence. Its query (queries/competency/cq-grade-axis-join-key.rq) also
# carries the fan-in witness in its body, so the roster empties the moment the rubric stops
# collapsing several axes onto one dimension — the condition that makes the key necessary.
ex:cqGradeAxisJoinKey a gmeow:CompetencyQuestion ;
    gmeow:cqQueryFile "slices/core/slice-quality-rubric/queries/competency/cq-grade-axis-join-key.rq" ;
    gmeow:cqRationale "A consumer holding a per-axis grade — a committed floor, a ratchet cell, an uplift report — must decide which axis it governs, and it has to find that key in the ontology rather than in a naming convention on the minted assessment IRI. Only one rubric-owned property carries it, so the roster is pinned BY IRI and not by a count: a count of one survives a rename or a substitution that quietly reroutes the join onto gmeow:qualityDimension, and that substitution is precisely the drift this question exists to catch, because sixteen axes publish under twelve dimensions and four dimensions each carry more than one axis. Rerouted, the ratchet would gate a slice on grades it never measured and would compare the meet of two axes against a floor committed for one."@x-gmeow-english ;
    gmeow:cqResultShape ex:cqGradeAxisJoinKeyShape ;
    gmeow:cqExactRows true ;
    gmeow:cqExpectRow ex:cqGradeAxisJoinKeyRow .

ex:cqGradeAxisJoinKeyShape a logic:ResultShape ;
    logic:declaresColumn
        [ logic:columnVariable "joinKey" ; logic:columnTermKind logic:TermKindIri ; logic:columnBinding logic:BindingRequired ] ;
    logic:shapeCardinality logic:RowsExact .

ex:cqGradeAxisJoinKeyRow a gmeow:ExpectedRow ;
    gmeow:rowCell [ a gmeow:ExpectedCell ; gmeow:cellVar "joinKey" ; gmeow:cellValueIri gmeow:assessmentAxis ] .
